# Inquisitor Module Rules

## The New Design

The Inquisitor is a **full-screen Akinator-style game**, not a two-column form. The user never sees a login screen before it — the dialogue is completely anonymous. Auth triggers only at execution time.

**Three states:**
1. Greeting — Stakinator character + intro bubble + "Let's go!"
2. Dialogue — question → answer → optional educational insert → repeat
3. Mandate card — plain-language profile summary + CTA

---

## lib/inquisitor/ — Starting Point, Not Gospel

`lib/inquisitor/` contains a **draft implementation** written during early conceptualisation. It has **not been tested, reviewed, or run**. Read it for design intent; rewrite anything that doesn't work.

Files and what to take from them:
- `types.ts` — mandate axes, distribution types → use as-is or restructure
- `questions.ts` — weight signals and question metadata → the 7-question educational pool below REPLACES the 20-question pool here; update this file
- `distribution.ts` — Bayesian update, entropy calculation → conceptually correct, verify edge cases
- `mandate.ts` — mandate builder, template deriver → use; update `mandateSummaryPrompt()` if needed
- `index.ts` — public API sketch → rewrite to match new session model

---

## The 7-Question Educational Pool

Replace the 20-question pool in `lib/inquisitor/questions.ts` with this pool:

```typescript
// Q1 — USDC literacy (always first)
// Q2 — ARC literacy (always second)
// Q3 — GOAL (always third)
// Q4 — USYC/yield education (always fourth; adapts text based on crypto_fluency)
// Q5 — RISK scenario (LLM-ordered, always shown)
// Q6 — HORIZON (LLM-ordered, always shown)
// Q7 — CAPITAL (LLM-ordered, always shown)
```

Each question has an optional `educationalInsert` field:
```typescript
type Question = {
  id: string;
  text: string;
  options: AnswerOption[];
  educationalInsert?: {
    triggerOptionId: string; // which answer triggers the insert
    title: string;
    body: string;           // 2-4 plain sentences
    learnMoreUrl?: string;  // external doc link
    continueLabel: string;  // button text e.g. "Got it! →"
  };
};
```

Educational inserts are triggered client-side — no extra API call needed.
The insert slides in, user reads, clicks continue, THEN the answer is submitted to API.

---

## Fixed vs LLM-Selected Questions

- **Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4**: Fixed order, always shown in this sequence
- **Q5, Q6, Q7**: LLM selects order after Q4 using `buildReasoningPrompt()`

The API for Q1–Q4 can return the next question directly (no LLM call needed).
Start calling Claude from Q5 onwards.

